  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThe surface front is draped across the area early this morning, from \nHiawatha to Abilene. With upper forcing displaced to the north, \nstorms have been forming along the 925-850mb front, which lies \nacross southern Nebraska. Showers and storms will slowly build south \nthrough the morning as the front advances through the area. Coverage \nof precipitation should continue to be scattered/widely scattered \nbefore increasing in coverage somewhat across east-central Kansas as \nthe boundary interacts with a more unstable pre-frontal environment. \nShear is still progged to be marginal (20kts), but SBCAPE of ~2000 \nJ/kg and high DCAPE values support the potential for damaging wind \ngusts, especially if any convection can evolve into clusters. \nHighest potential is along and southeast of Interstate 35 during the \nafternoon into the evening, generally 3-7 PM. The pre-frontal \nenvironment will still be hot with highs in the mid to upper 90s \nalong and south of the I-35 corridor. Dewpoints in the 70s will \ncreate heat index values of 100-105 degrees this afternoon, but \nvalues of this magnitude should be limited to a couple of hours \nbefore the front moves through and precipitation chances increase. \nAs such, will not issue any heat headlines.\n\nCoverage of precipitation increases again overnight tonight as the \nboundary hangs across southeast Kansas and isentropic ascent \nstrengthens. Storm motions will be largely parallel to the boundary \nwith high PWATs around 2\" leading to the potential for locally heavy \nrainfall. HREF members depict pockets of 1-3\" of rainfall across \neast-central Kansas into Thursday morning. Think the dry antecedent \nconditions will help limit flooding potential, but could have some \nlocalized flooding if storms do train over the same areas.\n\nPrecipitation comes to an end Thursday morning and skies clear from \nnorth to south. A much more comfortable day is expected with highs \nin the 80s. There is a low chance for some WAA showers/storms \nThursday night into Friday morning. Southerly flow returns on Friday \nand bumps temperatures back into mid 80s to low 90s. Models are\nmixed on the potential for an MCS to dive southeast into area \nFriday night into Saturday. The global models are much more aggressive\nthan the shorter-range models (RRFS and NAM) in generating a convective\ncomplex. With the NBM dominated by global models at this range, PoPs\nare quite highs, but there are signs that storms could be much \nless widespread. Additional rounds of precipitation move across \nthe area through the remainder of the weekend, although periods \nof dry time appear probable as well.\n\nThe stronger cold front that has been hinted at by guidance to move \nthrough Monday-Tuesday of next week has trended weaker and less \nprogressive, favoring warmer temperatures and higher precipitation \nchances as the front hangs across the region. Predictability is low \nas temperature spreads remain large, 10-15 degree spread in NBM 25th-\n75th percentiles.",
